{"i": 42, "key": "ig2b02:p6:light-cavalry-tail", "kind": "clause", "box": [71895, 66287, 487746, 97854], "label": "Light Cavalry continuation from page 5", "components": ["Light Cavalry continuation from page 5"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "cent Numidian light cavalry. They rarely carried any armor,\nother than a shield, or any other weapon."}
{"i": 43, "key": "ig2b02:p6:lancers:text-a", "kind": "clause", "box": [146241, 103535, 487746, 150253], "label": "Lancers (beside counter)", "components": ["Lancers inline bold heading", "Opening prose"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "Lancers: Essentially light cavalry units trained to\nuse a lance for attack. They have no missile capabil-\nity (a simplification for game purposes)."}
{"i": 44, "key": "ig2b02:p6:lancers:counter", "kind": "diagram", "box": [71895, 105429, 134804, 154041], "label": "Lancers sample counter", "components": ["Celts", "5", "4", "LN", "7", "8", "Mounted lancer illustration"], "uncertainty": ["Small counter glyphs are literal observations; exact transcription and semantic interpretation remain pending."], "text": ""}
{"i": 45, "key": "ig2b02:p6:heavy-cavalry:text-a", "kind": "clause", "box": [146241, 160984, 487746, 221591], "label": "Heavy Cavalry (beside counter)", "components": ["Heavy Cavalry inline bold heading", "Opening prose"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "Heavy Cavalry: Cavalry with some body armor,\nlight shields and, perhaps, a lance and/or sword. The\nheavy cavalry of the day was descended from Alex-\nander�s Companions, although they were nowhere"}
{"i": 46, "key": "ig2b02:p6:heavy-cavalry:counter", "kind": "diagram", "box": [71895, 165404, 134804, 213384], "label": "Heavy Cavalry sample counter", "components": ["Crthge", "3 SqdBnd (small abbreviation uncertain)", "5", "HC", "7", "8", "Mounted soldier illustration"], "uncertainty": ["Small counter glyphs are literal observations; exact transcription and semantic interpretation remain pending."], "text": ""}
{"i": 47, "key": "ig2b02:p6:heavy-cavalry:text-b", "kind": "clause", "box": [71895, 222222, 487746, 238006], "label": "Heavy Cavalry (below counter)", "components": ["Continued prose"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "near as effective."}
{"i": 48, "key": "ig2b02:p6:roman-cavalry:text-a", "kind": "clause", "box": [146241, 243686, 487746, 304293], "label": "Roman Cavalry (beside counter)", "components": ["Roman Cavalry inline bold heading", "Opening prose"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "Roman Cavalry: The Roman cavalry, including that\nof the alae, was in terms of weapons and armament,\nmore heavy than light in that they copied much from\nthe Greek era. However, they were neither trained in,"}
{"i": 49, "key": "ig2b02:p6:roman-cavalry:counter", "kind": "diagram", "box": [71895, 247474, 134804, 296086], "label": "Roman Cavalry sample counter", "components": ["I identifier", "3", "RC", "6", "8", "Roman mounted javelinist illustration"], "uncertainty": ["Small counter glyphs are literal observations; exact transcription and semantic interpretation remain pending."], "text": ""}
{"i": 50, "key": "ig2b02:p6:roman-cavalry:text-b", "kind": "clause", "box": [71895, 304924, 487746, 411617], "label": "Roman Cavalry (below counter)", "components": ["Continued prose"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "nor especially adept at, Shock Combat on the same level as\n�Heavy� cavalry, so they have received a different designation\nand a separate line on the Shock Superiority Chart, although they\nare treated as HC on the Clash of Spears and Swords Chart. The\nRomans never quite understood how to use cavalry and, although\nthey often had large numbers, they were rarely as effective as\none would expect."}
{"i": 51, "key": "ig2b02:p6:elephants:text-a", "kind": "clause", "box": [146241, 417297, 487746, 477905], "label": "Elephants (beside counter)", "components": ["Elephants inline bold heading", "Opening prose"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "Elephants: By this time, the sight of elephants on a\nbattlefield was common�to everybody, that is, ex-\ncept the Romans. Alexander�s descendants, the Di-\nadochi, had fallen in love with them, and a trained"}
{"i": 52, "key": "ig2b02:p6:elephants:counter", "kind": "diagram", "box": [71895, 421085, 134804, 469697], "label": "Elephants sample counter", "components": ["Africa", "15", "J/a missile glyph (uncertain)", "5", "EL", "5", "7", "Elephant/tower illustration"], "uncertainty": ["Small counter glyphs are literal observations; exact transcription and semantic interpretation remain pending."], "text": ""}
{"i": 53, "key": "ig2b02:p6:elephants:text-b", "kind": "clause", "box": [71895, 478535, 487746, 782197], "label": "Elephants (below counter)", "components": ["Continued prose"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "elephant corps was standard throughout the wars of Ptolemy,\nSeleucus, etc. The Romans, however, had not yet encountered\nthem when Pyrrhus arrived with his 20+ behemoths at Heraclea.\nThe result was typical: the Roman cavalry had a collective\ncoronary, and the Roman infantry didn�t wait around long enough\nto see their EKGs. By the time of Hannibal, however, the Romans\nwere pretty much used to them and had devised some ingenious\nmethods of negating their abilities; the effectiveness of jousting\nchariots and the flaming pigs of the Pyrrhic era having proved\nrather elusive. The elephants of Hannibal were the smaller, North\nAfrican forest variety�not the big Indian tuskers that Porus had\nat the Hydaspes (or, for that matter, the much larger southern\nAfrican variety)�and were usually equipped with towers, pur-\nportedly an invention of Pyrrhus. Pyrrhus� elephants, inherited\nfrom the Diadochi, were probably the bigger, Indian variety. War\nelephants were pretty much impervious to anything�except\nsharp, pointy objects and loud, strange noises. They also tended\nto have a mind of their own when annoyed, which often happened\nwhen confronted with those same sharp, pointy objects and\nstrange sounds."}
{"i": 54, "key": "ig2b02:p6:scorpio:text-a", "kind": "clause", "box": [146241, 787878, 487746, 848485], "label": "Scorpio (beside counter)", "components": ["Scorpio inline bold heading", "Opening prose"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "Scorpio: A Scorpio was a small, catapult-like engine\nthat was something like a crossbow on a tripod. It could\nshoot a large, spear-like bolt about 300-400 yards. The\n�artillery� was fairly standard issue to legions, and it"}
{"i": 55, "key": "ig2b02:p6:scorpio:counter", "kind": "diagram", "box": [71895, 791666, 134804, 840278], "label": "Scorpio sample counter", "components": ["Scorpio", "1 identifier", "LI", "1 red/yellow rating", "2", "3", "Artillery illustration"], "uncertainty": ["Small counter glyphs are literal observations; exact transcription and semantic interpretation remain pending."], "text": ""}
{"i": 56, "key": "ig2b02:p6:scorpio:text-b", "kind": "clause", "box": [71895, 849116, 487746, 880051], "label": "Scorpio (below counter)", "components": ["Continued prose"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "was used almost entirely for camp defense (the size, range and use\nof these early artillery pieces is open to much debate)."}
{"i": 57, "key": "ig2b02:p6:scale-heading", "kind": "heading", "box": [513071, 66287, 928922, 85228], "label": "2.5 GAME SCALE", "components": ["2.5 GAME SCALE"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "2.5 GAME SCALE"}
{"i": 58, "key": "ig2b02:p6:scale-hex-turn", "kind": "clause", "box": [513071, 85858, 928922, 117425], "label": "Hex distance and game-turn time", "components": ["Hex distance and game-turn time"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "Each hex is approximately 70+ paces (yards) from side to side.\nEach game turn covers about 20 minutes of real time."}
{"i": 59, "key": "ig2b02:p6:scale-size", "kind": "clause", "box": [513071, 123106, 928922, 275884], "label": "Size scale and skirmisher exception", "components": ["Size scale and skirmisher exception"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "Each point of Size denotes from 100-150 infantry (depending on\ntheir formation, rank depth and usage) or 100 cavalry. Thus, an\nAfrican Phalanx unit represents 1500+ men, a Roman Principes\nunit 300, and a Roman Heavy Cavalry unit about 300 men. Each\nRoman legion infantry unit is the equivalent of 2�3 maniples\n(granted, that�s an unwieldy division of labor; however, it makes\nfrontages realistic and, more importantly, the game playable).\nSkirmishers are an exception; their size reflects not their numeri-\ncal strength but their dispersed type of formation. Size, players\nwill soon realize, is the least important rating a unit has."}
{"i": 60, "key": "ig2b02:p6:scale-frontage", "kind": "clause", "box": [513071, 281565, 928922, 343435], "label": "Counter shape and frontage", "components": ["Counter shape and frontage"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "The counter shape of a unit represents its frontage. A Phalanx\nin full array had a frontage of approximately 150 yards; thus,\nit occupies 2 hexes. A cavalry unit had a frontage of about 50+\nyards, or one hex."}
{"i": 61, "key": "ig2b02:p6:length-heading", "kind": "heading", "box": [513071, 352904, 928922, 371844], "label": "2.6 GAME LENGTH", "components": ["2.6 GAME LENGTH"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "2.6 GAME LENGTH"}
{"i": 62, "key": "ig2b02:p6:length-withdrawal", "kind": "clause", "box": [513071, 372474, 928922, 404041], "label": "Battle duration and withdrawal", "components": ["Battle duration and withdrawal"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "Generally, battles are fought until one side withdraws, so there\nis usually no need to keep track of how many turns have passed."}
{"i": 63, "key": "ig2b02:p6:length-turn-track", "kind": "clause", "box": [513071, 409722, 928922, 456440], "label": "Game Turn Track", "components": ["Game Turn Track"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "A Game Turn Track has been provided to help track the timing\nof certain events (the entry of reinforcements, etc.) in some of\nthe battles."}
{"i": 64, "key": "ig2b02:p6:length-historical-note", "kind": "note", "box": [513071, 458964, 928922, 571970], "label": "Historical note on duration and casualties", "components": ["Historical note on duration and casualties"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "HISTORICAL NOTE: Roman battles took somewhat longer\nto complete than those of just 150 or so years previously.\nParticipants had developed a wide variety of tactical sys-\ntems to counter those of their enemy. In addition, armor and\nprotection were lighter and more efficient, enabling soldiers\nto fight longer. The result of all this was that casualties rose\nprecipitously, and battles lasted a great deal longer."}
{"i": 65, "key": "ig2b02:p6:latin-heading", "kind": "heading", "box": [513071, 585858, 928922, 605430], "label": "2.7 LATIN", "components": ["2.7 LATIN"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "2.7 LATIN"}
{"i": 66, "key": "ig2b02:p6:latin-prose", "kind": "clause", "box": [513071, 606060, 928922, 682450], "label": "Editorial Latin terminology discussion", "components": ["Editorial Latin terminology discussion"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "We have tried to use appropriate Latin military terms, as appli-\ncable. Six years of hacking one�s way through Caesar�s Com-\nmentaries finally came to some use. Memory, however, is a great\nvillain. I think we got most of the �endings� right; then again, if\nit came from the fifth declension, we probably �bluu� it."}
{"i": 67, "key": "ig2b02:p6:sequence-heading", "kind": "heading", "box": [513071, 700757, 928922, 727273], "label": "3.0 SEQUENCE OF PLAY", "components": ["3.0 SEQUENCE OF PLAY"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "3.0 SEQUENCE OF PLAY"}
{"i": 68, "key": "ig2b02:p6:sequence-introduction", "kind": "clause", "box": [513071, 727904, 928922, 804925], "label": "Sequence of Play introduction", "components": ["Sequence of Play introduction"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "Players activate each of their leaders in the order determined by\ntheir Initiative Rating. When a leader is activated, he issues orders\nwhich enable units to move and conduct combat. After all leaders\nare Finished, play proceeds to the Rout and Reload Phase, after\nwhich each player checks to see if his army withdraws."}
{"i": 69, "key": "ig2b02:p6:activation-heading", "kind": "heading", "box": [513071, 813762, 928922, 832703], "label": "A. Leader Activation Phase", "components": ["A. Leader Activation Phase"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "A. Leader Activation Phase"}
{"i": 70, "key": "ig2b02:p6:activation-prose", "kind": "clause", "box": [513071, 833964, 928922, 910985], "label": "Leader Activation Phase paragraph", "components": ["Leader Activation Phase paragraph"], "uncertainty": [], "text": "The player with the lowest Initiative-rated leader who has not yet\nbeen activated activates that leader. If a DR is required to enable\nthe activated leader to issue a Line Command, he rolls for such\nability at this point (4.33). Non-Momentum Trump attempts may\nbe made at this time (5.41)."}
